    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include \\\"knn.h\\\"\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    float dist;\\n    long idx;\\n    char label;\\n} HeapItem;\\n\\nstatic inline int worse(const HeapItem *a, const HeapItem *b) {\\n    if (a->dist > b->dist) return 1;\\n    if (a->dist < b->dist) return 0;\\n    return a->idx > b->idx;\\n}\\n\\nstatic inline void swap_items(HeapItem *a, HeapItem *b) {\\n    HeapItem t = *a;\\n    *a = *b;\\n    *b = t;\\n}\\n\\nstatic inline void sift_up(HeapItem *heap, int idx) {\\n    while (idx > 0) {\\n        int parent = (idx - 1) / 2;\\n        if (!worse(&heap[idx], &heap[parent])) break;\\n        swap_items(&heap[idx], &heap[parent]);\\n        idx = parent;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nstatic inline void sift_down(HeapItem *heap, int size, int idx) {\\n    for (;;) {\\n        int left = 2 * idx + 1;\\n        if (left >= size) break;\\n        int right = left + 1;\\n        int worst_child = left;\\n        if (right < size && worse(&heap[right], &heap[left])) {\\n            worst_child = right;\\n        }\\n        if (!worse(&heap[worst_child], &heap[idx])) break;\\n        swap_items(&heap[idx], &heap[worst_child]);\\n        idx = worst_child;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\\n    if (k <= 0) return '\\\\0';\\n\\n    // Count total points\\n    long N = 0;\\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; ++gi) {\\n        N += groups[gi].length;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Flatten point coordinates and labels into contiguous arrays\\n    float *xs = (float *)malloc((size_t)N * sizeof(float));\\n    float *ys = (float *)malloc((size_t)N * sizeof(float));\\n    char *plabels = (char *)malloc((size_t)N * sizeof(char));\\n\\n    long pos = 0;\\n    for (int gi = 0; gi < n_groups; ++gi) {\\n        Group g = groups[gi];\\n        char lab = g.label;\\n        Point *pts = g.points;\\n        int len = g.length;\\n        for (int j = 0; j < len; ++j) {\\n            xs[pos] = pts[j].x;\\n            ys[pos] = pts[j].y;\\n            plabels[pos] = lab;\\n            ++pos;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Compute squared distances in parallel\\n    float *dists = (float *)malloc((size_t)N * sizeof(float));\\n    float tx = to_evaluate.x;\\n    float ty = to_evaluate.y;\\n\\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\\n    for (long i = 0; i < N; ++i) {\\n        float dx = xs[i] - tx;\\n        float dy = ys[i] - ty;\\n        dists[i] = dx * dx + dy * dy;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Binary max-heap of size k keyed by (dist, idx)\\n    HeapItem *heap = (HeapItem *)malloc((size_t)k * sizeof(HeapItem));\\n    int counts[256] = {0};\\n    int heap_size = 0;\\n\\n    for (long i = 0; i < N; ++i) {\\n        float d = dists[i];\\n        char lab = plabels[i];\\n\\n        if (heap_size < k) {\\n            heap[heap_size].dist = d;\\n            heap[heap_size].idx = i;\\n            heap[heap_size].label = lab;\\n            counts[(unsigned char)lab]++;\\n            ++heap_size;\\n            sift_up(heap, heap_size - 1);\\n        } else if (d < heap[0].dist || (d == heap[0].dist && i < heap[0].idx)) {\\n            // New point is better than current worst\\n            counts[(unsigned char)heap[0].label]--;\\n            heap[0].dist = d;\\n            heap[0].idx = i;\\n            heap[0].label = lab;\\n            counts[(unsigned char)lab]++;\\n            sift_down(heap, k, 0);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Fill missing slots with label -1\\n    if (heap_size < k) {\\n        counts[(unsigned char)-1] += k - heap_size;\\n    }\\n\\n    free(xs);\\n    free(ys);\\n    free(plabels);\\n    free(dists);\\n    free(heap);\\n\\n    // Find majority label, scanning signed char order for tie-breaking\\n    char best = 0;\\n    int best_count = -1;\\n    for (int c = -128; c <= 127; ++c) {\\n        int cnt = counts[(unsigned char)c];\\n        if (cnt > best_count) {\\n            best_count = cnt;\\n            best = (char)c;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    return best;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\\n    \\n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\\n    }\\n\\n    int k = parse_k();\\n\\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%c\\\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/knn.c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Combining the binary max-heap top-k selection (A) with flattened contiguous arrays and parallel distance computation (B/C) should reduce complexity from O(N*k) to O(N log k + N/cores), improve cache locality, and preserve exact tie-breaking semantics via (distance, index) ordering.\",\n  \"notes\": \"Avoided posix_memalign and OpenMP aligned clauses that caused previous build failures. Uses plain malloc and a standard parallel-for loop for distance computation. Heap selection remains sequential but is O(N log k). Incremental label counts eliminate qsort on labels.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [\n    \"cc069d72d49241b4\",\n    \"aeef51b45ef54a22\",\n    \"922ffcaadda34be9\"\n  ],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Flatten group/point data into contiguous xs, ys, and label arrays for cache-friendly access.\",\n    \"Compute squared distances in parallel with OpenMP schedule(static).\",\n    \"Replace O(N*k) insertion-sort selection with a binary max-heap of size k keyed by (distance, arrival index), preserving original tie-breaking.\",\n    \"Maintain label frequency counts incrementally during heap insert/evict and choose the majority by scanning signed char order, eliminating qsort on labels.\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one bounded HPC source-code experiment. We are role G: integration of diverse archived transformations; evaluate the full program. We have three sources A, B, C with IDs cc069d72d49241b4 (A), aeef51b45ef54a22 (B), 922ffcaadda34be9 (C). Current source is baseline original knn.c and knn.h.\n\nWe need to integrate transformations from these sources into a new candidate. We must edit only src/knn.c and/or src/knn.h? The current_source_paths are [\"src/knn.c\", \"src/knn.h\"]. We can modify both if needed, but likely just knn.c. Need to preserve correctness.\n\nWe have automatic observations: baseline mean 2.085 s on ARM64 with OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 (cpu_cores=4). Previous own role experiments: one build failed due to posix_memalign and OpenMP aligned clause syntax; another valid but slow ~2.06s, essentially same as baseline. So we need a better integration.\n\nWe have three source transformations:\n\nA (cc069d72d49241b4): binary max-heap of size k keyed by (distance, arrival sequence), incremental label counts, no qsort. This is sequential O(N log k). It was measured mean 0.0273 s? Wait that's from the diff metadata: \"mean_seconds\": 0.02732969098724425 for A alone? That seems extremely fast compared to baseline 2s. But maybe those are on different hardware or smaller input? Actually these are archived transformations with their own measured times, likely on same benchmark? The baseline is ~2s, so A's 0.027s would be huge speedup (75x). B: OpenMP SIMD distance computation + stable max-heap top-k, mean 0.0314 s. C: flatten data, per-thread insertion-sorted lists with guided schedule, merge k-way, mean 0.109 s. So A and B are much faster than baseline. But our previous integration attempt (9b39f9afcf2447c6) combined binary max-heap + structure-of-arrays Group layout? Actually it used flattened aligned buffers and OpenMP SIMD distance computation plus heap; but measured ~2.06s, same as baseline. That suggests maybe the benchmark input is huge and memory allocation/parallel overhead dominates? Or perhaps our previous integration had a bug causing no speedup? Let's examine.\n\nThe current source uses original sequential insertion sort O(N*k) with k likely small? But baseline 2s indicates N large (maybe millions). A's heap approach should be much faster. Why did our previous valid experiment not improve? Possibly because we used OpenMP SIMD but the compiler didn't vectorize due to alignment issues, or we allocated huge arrays and memory bandwidth dominated. Or maybe the benchmark input is such that k is large? But still O(N log k) vs O(N*k). If k ~ 1000, heap should be faster.\n\nLet's inspect previous valid experiment details: It had sources b29cec62b6a945b9 (unknown), 3346fed8245e4b4e (unknown), and 922ffcaadda34be9 (C). So it integrated C? Actually the interaction list shows first attempt with c2445fbe50614c7a, 3346fed8245e4b4e, 922ffcaadda34be9 failed build; second with b29cec62b6a945b9, 3346fed8245e4b4e, 922ffcaadda34be9 valid but slow. Those source IDs are not in our integration_source_ids (only A,B,C). So previous experiments used different sources? The own_role_history shows experiment efd429023a2b424a build failed due to posix_memalign and OpenMP aligned clause; that likely integrated some of these. But the valid one 9b39f9afcf2447c6 had mean ~2.06s, essentially baseline.\n\nWe need a new integration using A, B, C ideas. The goal is speedup. We should combine best aspects: use binary max-heap (A) for O(N log k), flatten data into contiguous arrays (B/C) to improve cache and allow SIMD distance computation (B). But we must avoid previous pitfalls: no posix_memalign with OpenMP aligned clause syntax error; ensure correct pragma. Also maybe the benchmark is memory-bound, so parallelizing distance computation may not help if N huge but k small? Actually baseline 2s for sequential insertion sort O(N*k) suggests k might be large enough that heap helps.\n\nLet's think: The original algorithm loops over