g.label;\n        Point *pts = g.points;\n        int len = g.length;\n        for (int j = 0; j < len; ++j) {\n            xs[pos] = pts[j].x;\n            ys[pos] = pts[j].y;\n            plabels[pos] = lab;\n            ++pos;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Compute squared distances in parallel\n    float *dists = (float *)malloc((size_t)N * sizeof(float));\n    float tx = to_evaluate.x;\n    float ty = to_evaluate.y;\n\n#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (long i = 0; i < N; ++i) {\n        float dx = xs[i] - tx;\n        float dy = ys[i] - ty;\n        dists[i] = dx * dx + dy * dy;\n    }\n\n    // Binary max-heap of size k keyed by (dist, idx)\n    HeapItem *heap = (HeapItem *)malloc((size_t)k * sizeof(HeapItem));\n    int counts[256] = {0};\n    int heap_size = 0;\n\n    for (long i = 0; i < N; ++i) {\n        float d = dists[i];\n        char lab = plabels[i];\n\n        if (heap_size < k) {\n            heap[heap_size].dist = d;\n            heap[heap_size].idx = i;\n            heap[heap_size].label = lab;\n            counts[(unsigned char)lab]++;\n            ++heap_size;\n            sift_up(heap, heap_size - 1);\n        } else if (d < heap[0].dist || (d == heap[0].dist && i < heap[0].idx)) {\n            // New point is better than current worst\n            counts[(unsigned char)heap[0].label]--;\n            heap[0].dist = d;\n            heap[0].idx = i;\n            heap[0].label = lab;\n            counts[(unsigned char)lab]++;\n            sift_down(heap, k, 0);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Fill missing slots with label -1\n    if (heap_size < k) {\n        counts[(unsigned char)-1] += k - heap_size;\n    }\n\n    free(xs);\n    free(ys);\n    free(plabels);\n    free(dists);\n    free(heap);\n\n    // Find majority label, scanning signed char order for tie-breaking\n    char best = 0;\n    int best_count = -1;\n    for (int c = -128; c <= 127; ++c) {\n        int cnt = counts[(unsigned char)c];\n        if (cnt > best_count) {\n            best_count = cnt;\n            best = (char)c;\n        }\n    }\n\n    return best;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n_groups = parse_number_of_groups();\n    \n    Group * groups = (Group *) malloc(sizeof(Group) * n_groups);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        groups[i] = parse_next_group();\n    }\n\n    int k = parse_k();\n\n    Point to_evaluate = parse_point();\n\n    printf(\"%c\", knn(n_groups, groups, k, to_evaluate));\n}\n```\n\nCheck: In the replacement condition, we used `d < heap[0].dist || (d == heap[0].dist && i < heap[0].idx)`. This is equivalent to \"new item better than root\" where worse comparator says a worse if dist greater or equal and idx greater. So new better if d < root.dist OR (equal and i < root.idx). That's correct.\n\nBut note: we used `long` for N and indices; heap idx long, but sift functions use int size; k is int, fine.\n\nPotential issue: If N=0? Then loop doesn't run, heap_size=0, counts[-1] += k, best will be -1 (since only count >0). Original would have labels all -1 and qsort then majority -1. So output '\\0'? Actually char -1 is 0xFF, printf(\"%c\") prints byte 255 maybe not visible; but original does same. Fine.\n\nNow about OpenMP: We used `#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)`. This requires -fopenmp flag in Makefile. The baseline likely already compiles with OpenMP? The problem statement says use CC or CXX, and execution script sets OMP_NUM_THREADS. So the build system probably includes -fopenmp. We don't need to change Makefile (we are not allowed). So this should compile.\n\nBut we must ensure that if N is small, OpenMP overhead might hurt; but baseline already uses OpenMP? Not necessarily. However, our previous valid experiment used OpenMP and was slow anyway. But let's trust.\n\nWe could also consider using `#pragma omp simd` on the distance loop without parallel for to allow vectorization while keeping single thread? Actually we have 4 cores; parallel is good.\n\nOne concern: The heap selection sequential over N may be bottleneck if N huge and k large, but still O(N log k). Could also parallelize using per-thread heaps as C did, but that adds complexity. Given A alone measured 0.027s, sequential should be fine.\n\nLet's include sources_kept: We are integrating ideas from all three? The instruction says \"For integration retain only declared source ideas.\" and sources_kept field contains ONLY experiment IDs from integration_source_ids. So we can list all three if we use them. Our transformations combine A (heap), B (flatten + SIMD distance), C (flatten). We'll set sources_kept to [\"cc069d72d49241b4\", \"aeef51b45ef54a22\", \"922ffcaadda34be9\"].\n\nTransformations list: describe what we did.\n\nHypothesis: Combining binary max-heap selection with flattened contiguous arrays and parallel distance computation should reduce complexity from O(N*k) to O(N log k + N/cores) while improving cache locality, yielding significant speedup over baseline.\n\nNotes: mention avoiding previous pitfalls (no posix_memalign/aligned clause).\n\nNow produce JSON. Ensure content string has proper escaping for newlines as \\n in JSON. We'll output the file content with actual newline characters? In JSON, we need to encode line breaks as \\n. The response format says \"Encode line breaks separating source lines as JSON newlines.\" So we should put literal newline characters in the string? Actually JSON strings can contain escaped newlines `\\n`. We'll output a single string with actual newline characters? In many contexts, it's acceptable to include raw newlines inside JSON string if properly quoted? But strict JSON requires escaping. The instruction says \"Encode line breaks separating source lines as JSON newlines.\" That likely means use \\n escape sequences. So we'll produce content with `\\n` between lines.\n\nWe'll write the code in a single string with escaped newlines.\n\nLet's construct final answer."
